<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hosokawa Tadatoshi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1586–1641</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hosokawa Tadatoshi was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosokawa-tadaoki">Hosokawa Tadaoki</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokura">Kokura</a>, transferred in 1632 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumamoto">Kumamoto</a> in Higo with 540,000 koku after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> removed the Katō family. His family held Kumamoto until 1871. He took part in suppressing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimabara-rebellion">Shimabara Rebellion</a> and invited the swordsman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miyamoto-musashi">Miyamoto Musashi</a> to Kumamoto.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hosokawa-tadatoshi.jpg" alt="Hosokawa Tadatoshi" width="320" height="302" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yano Yoshishige (Inscription by Ōbuchi Genkō) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hosokawa_Tadatoshi_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
